<DocsLayout
  title="Lookout"
  description="A terminal dashboard over the shepherd: the flock table, a host-usage strip, and a selected sheep's detail pane and bleats feed."
  crumb="Interfaces / Lookout"
  activeSlug="lookout"
>
  <article>
    <h1>Lookout</h1>
    <ReferencePills slug="lookout" />
    <p class="lede">
      <code>shep lookout</code> (alias <code>dash</code>) is a live terminal
      dashboard over the shepherd. It reads the flock two ways at once: a
      subscription to the event bus so the screen moves as things happen, and
      a re-list every two seconds so a dropped event can't leave it quietly
      wrong.
    </p>

    <Callout variant="careful">
      Lookout needs a real terminal. With stdout redirected (piped to a file
      or another program) it refuses outright rather than writing escape
      sequences somewhere nobody will read them.
    </Callout>

    <Callout variant="note">
      If all you want is the flock table kept current, <code
        >shep flock --follow</code
      > redraws it in place with no panes and no action gate. Covered under <a
        href="/docs/output">terminal output</a
      >.
    </Callout>

    <h2>Start it</h2>
    <CodeBlock>{startCmds}</CodeBlock>
    <p class="fine">
      <code>--read-only</code> closes the action gate, covered below. The
      gate is open by default.
    </p>

    <h2>The flock table</h2>
    <p>
      Every frame below is real: rendered headlessly through ratatui's{" "}
      <code>TestBackend</code> by a pinned snapshot test, not mocked up for
      this page. A <code>&gt;</code> gutter to the left of ID marks the
      selected sheep. <code>j</code>/<code>k</code> move the selection one
      row at a time; <code>g</code>/<code>G</code> jump to the first and last
      row. Everything below the table describes whichever sheep is selected.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="120×30">{healthy}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      Rows sit under two reverse-video bands, <code>FLOCK</code> for the
      sheep and <code>DOGS</code> below it for anything shep is supervising
      as a dog. Each band shows up only when its side has a row.
    </p>

    <h2>Multi-instance apps</h2>
    <p>
      An app with more than one instance gets a group row above its slots,
      the same rollup <code>shep flock</code> shows: <code>web
      &times;3</code> in NAME, STATUS as the shared word or a count per state
      when the instances disagree, RESTARTS/CPU/MEM summed and UPTIME the
      minimum across them. The group row is selectable with <code>j</code>/
      <code>k</code> like any other row, and the cursor survives the
      two-second poll on either kind of row.
    </p>
    <p>
      An action key on a selected group row is the one place in lookout a
      single keypress reaches more than one process, so the confirm states
      how many it's about to touch:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock>{`stop all 3 instances of web? enter confirms, any other key cancels`}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      <code>Enter</code> sends the action against the whole app by name, not
      against one pinned id, and the detail pane on a group row shows the
      app-level summary rather than a single process's fields. The bleats
      feed can't follow a group, since there's no single log to re-read for
      it: it names the app and asks you to select one instance instead.
    </p>

    <h2>Grouping by fold</h2>
    <p>
      <code>F</code> gathers the table by fold, <code>AppConfig::fold</code>,
      instead of by name. Each fold gets a header row; sheep with no fold sit
      under a <code>no fold</code> band, and dogs, which are never in a fold,
      keep their own <code>Dogs</code> band. <code>F</code> again returns to
      the flat table.
    </p>
    <p>
      A triangle marks each fold header: ▾ when its members are showing,
      ▸ when <code>z</code> has collapsed it. Member rows carry none, so
      the two kinds of row stay apart with every colour stripped.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="160×30, fold view">{folds}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      A fold header's restarts, CPU and memory are its members summed. Uptime
      is the exception: it's the shortest of the members', so the header
      reads as time since the fold was last disturbed rather than the age of
      its longest-lived sheep. The SHARE gauge and the NOTES percentage both
      show that fold's share of the whole flock's memory. A multi-instance
      app inside a fold still gets its own group row, the same <code>web
      &times;3</code> rollup it would draw in the flat table, so a fold never
      nests three levels deep.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>z</code> collapses the fold under the cursor, hiding its members
      and leaving the header's own rollup on screen. Pressed again it opens
      back up. It does nothing on the <code>no fold</code> or
      <code>Dogs</code> bands, or anywhere outside the fold view.
    </p>
    <p>
      An action key on a fold header reaches every sheep in it, the same way
      a group row's does, and the confirm names the count:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock>{`restart all 4 sheep in fold edge? enter confirms, any other key cancels`}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      The <code>no fold</code> header isn't selectable. There's no selector
      that names "everything with no fold" on the wire, so an action there
      would have nothing to send.
    </p>

    <h2>The three panes underneath</h2>
    <p>
      The host strip is the line above the table: load average and host
      memory each draw a ten-cell gauge, load against the core count and
      memory against the host's total, with the gauge coming before its
      numbers. It also carries how many sheep are errored or parked, the
      flock's own summed CPU and memory, and how long the machine's been up.
      At 120 columns the frame above only has room for the first three; flock
      CPU, flock memory and uptime crowd off the edge before you can read
      them. Below the table,
      the detail pane restates the selected row in full sentences and adds
      what the row can't fit: the untruncated name and both log paths.
    </p>
    <p>
      The detail pane also shows the selected sheep's lambs: a count of the
      descendants <code>Describe</code>'s process-table walk found, each
      one's pid and executable name, and how long ago that reading was
      taken. It's fetched once when the selection actually moves onto a
      running sheep, not on the table's own two-second listing, so the age
      stamp is real: hold the cursor on one sheep and the lamb list gets
      stale rather than silently refreshing behind a timestamp that still
      says "just now". A stopped sheep has no pid to walk from, and the pane
      says that plainly rather than reporting zero lambs.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="120×30, with lambs">{lambs}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      The bleats feed is the one pane that isn't built from the flock
      listing: it re-reads the selected sheep's <code>out</code>/
      <code>err</code> log files from disk on every refresh, rather than
      subscribing to the bus. That's a deliberate cost: a busy flock pays one
      bounded 64&nbsp;KiB read per file per refresh, instead of making the
      dashboard the highest-volume subscriber on the bus for a pane most
      refreshes don't even redraw. When it can't show everything, it says so
      instead of guessing. Lines it read and dropped are counted exactly,
      and bytes below its window are reported in bytes, because nothing
      counted the lines inside them:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock>{feedExcerpt}</CodeBlock>

    <h2>The sheep pane</h2>
    <p>
      <code>↵</code> on a selected sheep opens a full-screen pane for it.
      Nothing happens on a group row, a fold header, or a dog: there's no
      single process to chart or config to show. <code>esc</code> closes it
      back to the table.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two charts share a time axis: CPU history above, memory RSS below,
      one column per two-second poll. The window is whatever the terminal's
      width buys, stated in the chart's own header rather than promised as a
      constant: a fresh pane reads <code>collecting</code> until it has
      enough polls to fill its own body. The memory chart draws a ceiling
      line at <code>max_memory</code> when the sheep has one; with none set
      it scales to the window's own peak instead and says so.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="160×48, the design size">{sheepPane}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      Below the charts, a read-only listing of the sheep's config and env
      keys sits beside a <code>BLEATS</code> feed scoped to this sheep.{" "}
      <code>e</code> opens the same full editor the dashboard's own{" "}
      <code>e</code> does; <code>b</code> hands the embedded feed to the
      full-screen bleats pane, filter and all, rather than opening a fresh
      one; <code>/</code> filters it in place. <code>J</code>/<code>K</code>{" "}
      step to the next or previous sheep in the current sort and filter,
      re-fetching its config and re-scaling both charts without leaving the
      pane. <code>x</code>, <code>R</code> and <code>L</code> arm and
      confirm against the pinned sheep, the same gate and the same prompt
      the dashboard's own action keys use.
    </p>
    <p>
      Narrower terminals give up the second chart before the first: below
      140 columns the memory chart folds into one line, an RSS reading and
      a ten-cell gauge; below 100 both charts fall back to the flock
      table's own <code>CPU 20s</code> sparkline and <code>MEM/CEIL</code>{" "}
      gauge, one row. The config and feed columns are what the pane is for,
      so they give up ground last: the memory chart drops below 26 rows,
      the CPU chart below 21.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="99×48, the sparkline fallback">{sheepPaneSparklines}</CodeBlock>

    <h2>The bleats pane, full screen</h2>
    <p>
      <code>b</code> opens the selected sheep's feed full screen and pins it
      there. There's no table on screen anymore to change the selection
      with, so the pane keeps describing that one sheep until it closes. It
      reads the same log files the corner feed does, just polled faster
      while it has focus.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three filters stack on top of the feed, and a line has to pass all
      three to show. <code>o</code> cycles the stream (out, err, or both),{" "}
      <code>m</code> cycles a minimum level, and <code>/</code> opens a box
      for a text or regex match, with matches highlighted. A level is a
      bare word like <code>WARN</code>, or a <code>level=warn</code> pair,
      so the filter works on logfmt output too. An app that writes neither
      can declare its own patterns, with{" "}
      <a href="/docs/logs"><code>level_rules</code></a> in its Flockfile. A
      line with no detectable level always shows, whatever the minimum is:
      most app output carries no level word at all, and hiding it would
      defeat the reason the pane exists. The filter row names which axes are
      set and counts how many lines in the current window survive them.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="100×14, filtered and wrapped">{bleatsFull}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      <code>esc</code> drops the newest filter first, one at a time, and
      only closes the pane once none are left. <code>j</code>/<code>k</code>{" "}
      scroll a line, <code>ctrl-d</code>/<code>ctrl-u</code> a page,{" "}
      <code>G</code> jumps to the end and starts following again,{" "}
      <code>f</code> toggles following, <code>w</code> wraps long lines
      instead of truncating them, and <code>n</code>/<code>N</code> step
      between matches.
    </p>

    <h2>Filtering the table</h2>
    <p>
      <code>/</code> opens a query box in the status bar; typing narrows the
      table to sheep whose name contains it, case-insensitively, as you type.{" "}
      <code>Enter</code> applies the query and closes the box, <code>Esc</code>{" "}
      clears it back to the whole flock, and both leave the cursor on a real
      row: if the sheep it was on drops out of the narrowed set, the
      selection moves to whatever now sits in the same position rather than
      pointing at a row the table has stopped drawing. The title always
      carries the flock's true size beside the filtered count, so a narrow
      query can't be read as a small flock, and the host strip above the
      table keeps summing the whole flock too, not just the two rows the
      filter leaves on screen, because it describes the machine, not the
      table's current view. At 100 columns the strip's gauges crowd those
      totals off the edge before you can read them, but the filter still
      hasn't touched what they'd be summing.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="100×14, filter applied">{filterActive}</CodeBlock>

    <h2>When the terminal is small</h2>
    <p>
      Panes drop before columns do, least-diagnostic-first: below 24 rows the
      detail pane goes (everything on it but the log paths already sits in
      the row above it). Below 18 the feed goes with it, and below 14 the
      host strip goes too, leaving only the flock table. Within the table,
      narrow widths drop columns one at a time, in this order:{" "}
      <code>MEM/CEIL</code>, <code>CPU 20s</code>, CFG, SMIT, FOLD, EXIT,
      RESTARTS, PID, MEM, CPU, and finally UPTIME, leaving{" "}
      <code>ID NAME STATUS</code> as the floor. <code>MEM/CEIL</code> is a
      gauge of a sheep's memory against its ceiling and <code>CPU 20s</code>{" "}
      is a sparkline of its CPU history; both restate a number CPU and MEM
      already carry, so they go first. A terminal needs 148 columns for the
      gauge and 136 for the sparkline, so the only frame on this page wide
      enough to draw either is the frozen one further down. Every row's
      sparkline scales to the same
      ceiling, the busiest sample any sheep has posted in the retained
      window, so rows read against each other rather than each one filling
      its own column.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      <code>CFG</code> drops third, right after <code>MEM/CEIL</code> and{" "}
      <code>CPU 20s</code> and still ahead of <code>SMIT</code>, which is
      not where the <a href="/docs/output">CLI table</a> puts it. The frames
      on this page are 120 columns wide, and tying <code>CFG</code> to{" "}
      <code>EXIT</code>'s tier the way that table does would have pushed the
      full set past 120 and quietly taken <code>SMIT</code> off every frame
      in the gallery. Dropping <code>CFG</code> on its own leaves a
      120-column terminal showing exactly what it showed before the column
      existed; you see <code>CFG</code> past 122.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="51×14">{narrow}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      The settings screen has its own drop order, and its two tables
      disagree about which column goes first. The scalar rows drop the
      apply cost and keep SOURCE; the dogs table drops SOURCE and keeps
      RUNNING. Each keeps whichever fact is not said anywhere else on the
      screen. The apply cost already appears twice more, in the confirm and
      in the status bar the moment a candidate arms, so it can go first.
      SOURCE on a scalar row is the only place that says whether an
      operator wrote the value or shep assumed it, so it stays until the
      last column falls. The dogs table has no such backup for SOURCE, so
      it goes, and RUNNING, the one fact nothing else on the pane answers,
      stays.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="45×24">{settingsNarrow}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      Below 33 columns or 6 rows, lookout refuses to draw rather than risk
      overlapping garbage. The refusal message is short enough to survive
      the terminal it's warning about:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="28×8">{tooNarrow}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      The secrets pane has a floor of its own, at 74 columns. Its narrowest
      tier is <code>KEY</code>, <code>VALUE</code> and <code>IN FORCE</code>,
      and a key with no value and no scope answers nothing, so there is
      nothing narrower to fall back to. Below 74 the pane says so, and the
      rest of lookout still draws at 33.
    </p>

    <h2>If the shepherd goes away</h2>
    <p>
      Lookout re-dials five times (at 250/500/1000/2000/4000&nbsp;ms, about
      7.75 seconds total) before it gives up. Then it stops polling and
      leaves the last known values on screen, with the band saying so and
      the whole table in one muted grey: nothing there is current, so
      nothing there is allowed to look current. <code>UPTIME</code> becomes
      <code>FROZEN</code>, because a clock still ticking after everything
      around it stopped would be lying about a specific sheep by name.
    </p>
    <p>
      The sheep detail band and the bleats feed hand their rows to a link
      panel. Both describe a shepherd that is gone; the panel describes
      what happened to it, quoting the last dial's own error. Lookout never
      exits on its own. <code>q</code> quits, <code>r</code> dials again,
      and every other key refuses while the link is down.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="160×30, shepherd dead">{frozen}</CodeBlock>

    <h2>Actions</h2>
    <p>
      <code>--read-only</code> (or <code>shep set lookout.allow_control
      false</code>) closes the gate; it's open by default, and closed means
      every action key refuses with a plain sentence rather than silently
      doing nothing:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="120×30, gate closed">{refused}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      With the gate open, three keys arm an action on the selected sheep:{" "}
      <code>x</code> stops it, <code>R</code> restarts it, <code>L</code>{" "}
      reloads it. There's no <code>start</code> key; lookout only ever acts
      on a sheep already in the flock. Arming doesn't send anything by
      itself, it puts a question in the status bar naming the verb and the
      exact sheep:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="100×14, armed">{confirm}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      <code>Enter</code> confirms and sends; any other key cancels and does
      nothing else, so a stray <code>j</code> right after arming can't both
      dismiss the prompt and move the cursor. An armed prompt nobody answers
      expires after 10 seconds on its own. Once sent, the bar shows the
      request in flight until the shepherd answers, either with what it did:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="100×14, accepted">{actionAccepted}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      or with its own refusal, forwarded rather than rewritten, so the
      sentence on screen is the daemon's, not lookout's guess at one. Besides
      the closed gate, arming also refuses if the link isn't live, if nothing
      is selected, or if another action is already out: one at a time, and
      the ladder is checked in that order every time.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is a fat-finger catch, not a security boundary: lookout runs as
      your own process under your own uid, so the shepherd has no way to
      refuse a keystroke it can't tell apart from you typing{" "}
      <code>shep stop</code> yourself. Every color on screen is also
      redundant with text, so <code>NO_COLOR</code> and a 16-color terminal
      lose decoration, never information.
    </p>
    <h2>Settings</h2>
    <p>
      <code>s</code> opens a full-screen settings view over{" "}
      <code>shep.toml</code>: the four daemon fields, the whistle gate, the
      style level, and the dogs table's own enable/disable, each with a
      SOURCE column and a note on what it takes to apply. <code>s</code>{" "}
      or <code>Esc</code> closes it again, back to the flock table.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reading this screen needs nothing, gate open or closed, the same as
      everything else on the dashboard. Changing a value is a
      different story: every edit and the dog toggle need the same gate the
      action keys do, and refuse the same way if it's closed.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="120×30, a fresh $SHEP_HOME">{settingsFresh}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      SOURCE reads <code>the default</code> when <code>shep.toml</code>{" "}
      doesn't mention the field at all, and the VALUE column shows the
      compiled fallback instead of anything from disk. A fresh{" "}
      <code>$SHEP_HOME</code> ships a <code>shep.toml</code> holding only{" "}
      <code>[interpreters]</code>, so this is the state you land in the
      first time you open the screen: every field on it is a guess about
      what the daemon assumes, not a record of what anyone wrote. Once a
      field is set, SOURCE switches to <code>shep.toml</code> and VALUE
      shows what's actually there.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="120×30, some fields set">{settingsDogs}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      The <code>[dogs]</code> table is the one place SOURCE isn't the whole
      story: IN FILE says whether <code>[daemon] enabled_dogs</code> names
      the dog, SOURCE says whether it's built in or adopted from a path, and
      RUNNING joins both against the live flock. The three can disagree, and
      when they do that's the point of showing all three: <code>otel</code>{" "}
      above is running with nothing enabling it, <code>ledger</code> is
      enabled and not running, and <code>bark</code> is enabled, running,
      and still <code>silent</code> because it never finished a handshake.{" "}
      <code>space</code> arms a toggle on the selected dog and <code>Enter</code>{" "}
      sends it; unlike a scalar edit, this reaches the shepherd directly and
      needs no reload.
    </p>
    <Callout variant="careful">
      A <code>[daemon]</code> edit only ever changes <code>shep.toml</code>.
      The shepherd itself can be started with <code>SHEP_LOG_LEVEL</code>,{" "}
      <code>SHEP_SOCKET</code>, or the matching <code>--log-level</code> /
      <code>--socket</code> flags, and shep's own precedence puts those above
      the file. Lookout has no way to see what the shepherd was booted with,
      so setting <code>log_level</code> here and running{" "}
      <code>shep daemon reload</code> can do nothing at all if the process
      was started with <code>SHEP_LOG_LEVEL</code> set, and nothing on
      screen will say so. <code>[style]</code> is not an exception:{" "}
      <code>--style</code> and <code>$SHEP_STYLE</code> outrank the file
      there too. The difference is that those layers belong to lookout's own
      process, so lookout can see them. SOURCE names the one in force, the
      last column reads <code>written, but outranked</code> instead of
      promising anything, and the confirm names the layer that will keep
      winning.
    </Callout>
    <p>
      The confirm names exactly this risk when it's <code>[daemon]</code>{" "}
      that's armed:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="180×30, armed">{settingsConfirm}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      <code>log_level</code>, <code>log_json</code>, and{" "}
      <code>allow_control</code> cycle through a fixed set of values, and the
      cycle only ever moves a field from <code>the default</code> to{" "}
      <code>shep.toml</code>, never back: <code>space</code> proposes the
      next value, it's never "unset". <code>socket</code> and{" "}
      <code>max_cron_sleep</code> are the only two fields this screen can put
      back to the default, because they're free text, and clearing the
      editor back to an empty line is what unsets them. <code>style level</code>{" "}
      is optional too, but <code>shep style</code> is what owns it and can't
      clear it either, so this screen doesn't offer to.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keys, all of them local to this screen while it's open:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>s</code> opens the screen, and closes it again</li>
      <li><code>j</code>/<code>k</code>, arrows move the cursor one row</li>
      <li><code>g</code>/<code>G</code> jump to the first or last row</li>
      <li>
        <code>space</code> arms the cursor's row; pressing it again on the
        same field re-arms with the next candidate
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>Enter</code> opens the text editor on <code>socket</code>/
        <code>max_cron_sleep</code>, or sends an armed confirm
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>Esc</code> cancels an armed confirm, else abandons the text
        editor, else closes the screen; it never quits lookout
      </li>
      <li><code>r</code> re-reads <code>shep.toml</code> from disk</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="fine">
      An armed candidate is cancelled, not acted on, by any movement key, by{" "}
      <code>r</code>, or by <code>s</code>: the same rule the dashboard's own
      action keys follow, where a stray keystroke right after arming backs
      out of the question instead of both dismissing it and doing something
      else. The confirm itself renders in the status bar, the same fixed row
      the dashboard's <code>x</code>/<code>R</code>/<code>L</code> confirm
      uses.
    </p>
    <h2>Secrets</h2>
    <p>
      <code>S</code>, <code>s</code>'s neighbour, opens a full-screen view
      over the store <a href="/docs/secrets">the secrets page</a> covers:
      the operator's own keys from <code>secrets.json</code> and each
      provider's from <code>secrets-cache.json</code>, one tab per
      environment. <code>Esc</code> or <code>S</code> again closes it.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="160×48">{secretsFrame}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      Two gates, and neither implies the other. Revealing a value needs{" "}
      <code>[secrets] allow_read = true</code> in <code>shep.toml</code>,
      off by default; changing anything (setting a key, deleting one, or
      adding a new one) needs the same{" "}
      <code>lookout.allow_control</code> gate the dashboard's action keys
      do, covered above. A read-only lookout with the reveal gate on can
      look but not touch; a controlling lookout without it can rotate a
      key it can never read back.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>S</code> opens the pane, and closes it again</li>
      <li>
        <code>&larr;</code>/<code>&rarr;</code>, or <code>Tab</code>/{" "}
        <code>Shift-Tab</code>, move the environment tab
      </li>
      <li><code>j</code>/<code>k</code>, <code>g</code>/<code>G</code> move the cursor</li>
      <li><code>z</code> collapses the group under the cursor, hiding its members</li>
      <li>
        <code>v</code> reveals the selected row's value for ten seconds,
        gated on <code>allow_read</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>Enter</code> opens a value input on an operator row, or
        confirms an armed delete
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>D</code> arms deleting the selected key; like every other
        armed key in lookout, it expires after ten seconds unanswered,{" "}
        <code>Enter</code> is what actually confirms it, and any other key
        cancels it
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>y</code> sends an already-revealed value to the terminal over
        OSC 52
      </li>
      <li><code>r</code> reloads the store</li>
      <li><code>q</code> quits lookout</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="fine">
      A reveal clears on far more than its own timeout: moving the
      selection, changing tabs, pressing <code>Esc</code>, reloading with{" "}
      <code>r</code>, closing the pane, quitting, or a write landing all
      drop it early, so a value never sits on screen describing a row it
      no longer belongs to.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      <code>y</code> only says the value was sent to the terminal, never
      that it arrived: OSC 52 has no acknowledgement, and the system
      clipboard is readable by whatever else is running on the desktop, a
      wider exposure than the screen it just left. A provider row refuses
      both <code>Enter</code> and <code>D</code> outright; it's a cache of
      what a dog pushed, not something lookout can write back.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      A row taking its value from the <code>all</code> slot refuses{" "}
      <code>D</code> on a named tab: removing that slot changes every
      environment at once, so the <code>all</code> tab is where you do it
      deliberately. A delete that finds no value to remove says so rather
      than reporting a success it did not have.
    </p>
    <h2>The config pane</h2>
    <p>
      <code>e</code> on a selected sheep opens its config over the dashboard:
      every Flockfile field, the value in force, and what changing it would
      cost. Fields sit under eight groups: process, logging, inputs,
      restart, readiness, shutdown, watch, cron. It writes as well, behind
      the same gate. Under{" "}
      <code>--read-only</code> the first key that would file an edit refuses
      the same way <code>x</code> does; the pane stays open and unchanged. A
      row marked <code>=</code> or <code>~</code> gives its own reason
      instead, in both states, because the flag would not have helped it.
    </p>
    <p>
      No edit reaches the shepherd until the pane closes. An edit is filed
      into a change set the pane carries, not sent, and <code>Esc</code>{" "}
      writes the whole set at once when it closes the pane. <code>u</code>{" "}
      undoes the most recently filed edit, one at a time, and files nothing
      itself. <code>r</code> is not an edit: it re-reads the pane's config
      from the shepherd while the pane is still open, same as any other
      request.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      A field whose grammar accepts a bare number, such as{" "}
      <code>max_memory</code> or <code>kill_timeout</code>, shows its
      resolved value rather than the digits on disk: <code>64</code> reads
      as <code>64 B</code>, and <code>1600</code> as <code>1600ms</code>,
      because a bare <code>max_memory</code> is bytes and a bare timer is
      milliseconds, neither of which is what most operators expect. The
      grammar itself is unchanged; only what the row draws is.
    </p>
    <p>Two markers can sit in front of a key, and the pane's own key hint
      names both, so the meaning is on screen wherever the pane is:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>*</code> an operator has overridden it</li>
      <li>
        <code>!</code> it is parked until the next respawn, so the running
        child is not holding it yet
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>And one in the column before those, when the pane won't edit a field:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>=</code> shep refuses a config write to it anywhere, not just
        here. That is <code>name</code> and <code>instances</code>, and the
        cost column says <code>read-only</code> to match.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>~</code> the pane has no widget for the shape, and nothing
        more. A Flockfile writes these the usual way, so the cost column
        beside them reports a real cost rather than <code>read-only</code>.
        Two fields: <code>liveness_probe</code> and{" "}
        <code>readiness_probe</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="fine">
      Both are glyphs rather than a colour on purpose. The narrowest terminal
      the pane draws into has already dropped the cost column, and{" "}
      <code>plain</code> style has no colour to read, so a greyed-out row
      would be the only signal left in the case where it says least.
    </p>
    <p>
      The last column is the field's class, which is what a change to it
      usually costs:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>now</code> the shepherd re-reads it at the next decision</li>
      <li>
        <code>next start</code> it reaches the next process this sheep spawns
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>respawn</code> the running child holds it, so that instance has
        to be replaced
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>read-only</code> identity or flock shape rather than a runtime
        knob. That is <code>name</code> and <code>instances</code>; the count
        moves with <code>shep stock</code>, not from here.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="fine">
      Read it before you write, not after. It says what kind of field this
      is, and a particular write can land differently: <code>watch</code>{" "}
      says <code>now</code> and waits for a reload if the{" "}
      <code>cwd</code> it needs is itself still parked, and{" "}
      <code>autostart</code> says <code>next start</code> and takes effect
      immediately, because shep reads it at muster. The status bar after the
      write is the one that reports what actually happened, and the{" "}
      <code>!</code> flag on the row carries it afterwards.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      <code>cwd</code>, <code>script</code>, <code>out_file</code> and{" "}
      <code>err_file</code> get one more line on that status bar when the
      shepherd looks at the path and it looks wrong: a directory that
      isn't there, or a script it can't find. The write still lands. A
      deploy that hasn't created the directory yet is a real config, not a
      typo, so this is a warning next to the write rather than a refusal
      of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      A tab row above the fields names all eight groups. <code>Tab</code>{" "}
      walks to the next one, and <code>1</code> through <code>8</code> jump
      straight to a group by its position in that row. Either resets the
      cursor to the group's first field.
    </p>
    <p>
      A panel to the right of a wide enough pane explains whichever field
      the cursor is on: its help text, its current value, its default, an
      example, what it accepts and refuses, and which other fields it
      interacts with. <code>h</code> still shows the same field's help text
      inline for a narrower terminal, since the panel needs room the field
      list doesn't. Below 90 columns there's no room for it and the cost
      column carries every row's cost alone. From 90 up the panel draws and
      the cost column gives way to it, since the panel already names the
      focused field's own cost. From 160 up the pane is wide enough for
      both at once.
    </p>
    <p>Keys, local to the pane while it's open:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>e</code> opens the pane from the dashboard</li>
      <li><code>j</code>/<code>k</code>, arrows move the cursor one row</li>
      <li><code>g</code>/<code>G</code> jump to the first or last field</li>
      <li>
        <code>r</code> re-reads the config and leaves the cursor where it was
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>space</code> files the other value for a bool, the next one
        for a choice, or the next suggestion for <code>kill_signal</code>{" "}
        and <code>cron_restart</code>, straight into the change set. Press it
        again to walk another step. A suggestion is not a closed list:{" "}
        <code>e</code> still types anything else.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>e</code> or <code>Enter</code> opens the editor on a text or
        number field; a second <code>e</code>/<code>Enter</code> files
        whatever was typed. Two keystrokes to change a field, never one.
        Inside the pane the two keys do exactly the same thing, so learning
        either is enough.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>e</code> or <code>Enter</code> on an array field opens a list
        sub-screen instead: <code>d</code> removes the selected element,{" "}
        <code>K</code>/<code>J</code> move it, and <code>e</code>/
        <code>Enter</code> edits it, or adds one from the{" "}
        <code>+ new</code> row. Each of those files the whole array on the
        spot; there's no second key to send it. They build on each other,
        and the rows show what you have filed rather than what the shepherd
        sent, so you can remove two elements or add several before leaving.
        <code>u</code> in here drops the array's whole entry and puts the
        shepherd's own back.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>d</code> on the field list restores the selected field to its
        default, filing that too rather than sending it. A field already at
        its default, or one shep refuses to write, files nothing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>u</code> undoes the most recently filed edit, one at a time.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>Tab</code> moves to the next group; <code>1</code> through{" "}
        <code>8</code> jump straight to one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>h</code> shows the selected field's own help text under the
        title. Press it again, or <code>Esc</code>, to dismiss it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>Esc</code> closes whatever is innermost: a sub-screen back to
        the field list, then help if it's open, then a question if anything
        the running child hasn't taken is outstanding, and last the pane
        itself, writing the whole change set as it closes. It never quits
        lookout.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Every filed edit is recorded as yours once it's sent: the key picks up
      the <code>*</code> marker here and in <code>shep flock</code>'s{" "}
      <code>CFG</code> column, and a later <code>shep start</code> of the
      app's Flockfile leaves it alone. Nothing else in the config moves,{" "}
      <code>instances</code> included.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      A field a write cannot reach in the running child parks until the next
      spawn, since the child was given its value at exec and holds it. If
      an edit still filed needs a respawn, or a field is already parked from
      an earlier write, <code>Esc</code> asks before it closes: restart now,
      reload, or leave it running with the edits parked. <code>L</code>{" "}
      reloads the sheep, <code>R</code> restarts it, <code>c</code> writes
      and leaves it running, and <code>Esc</code> again keeps editing and
      writes nothing. The restart or reload only fires once its write has
      landed, so it never respawns into the config it was meant to replace.
      The dialog shows up only with the gate open. <code>shep reload
      &lt;sheep&gt;</code> promotes an already-parked field from the CLI,
      any time after.
    </p>
    <p>
      A sheep's env keys sit in the field list itself, beneath whichever
      group is on screen, rather than behind their own row. No value ever
      appears there, because the shepherd never sends one: every key reads
      {" "}
      <code>(set)</code>. <code>e</code> or <code>Enter</code> on a
      key types a new value for it, and filing an empty one removes the
      key. Either on the <code>+ new</code> row takes{" "}
      <code>KEY=value</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      An env write files the same way a field does, a set as much as a
      removal, and waits with everything else for the pane to close. The
      old value is overwritten in the store as soon as the write lands, and
      this screen cannot read a value back to put it there again, so an
      overwrite loses as much as a delete does.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      env is spawn-time in every case, so nothing set here reaches the running
      child until that child is replaced.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      A group row opens one too. Every instance of a multi-instance app
      shares one stored spec, so the pane is about the app rather than about
      whichever instance the cursor was over.
    </p>

    <h2>The dog config pane</h2>
    <p>
      <code>e</code> on a dog row opens its config, over that dog's{" "}
      <code>[&lt;name&gt;]</code> section in <code>dogs.toml</code>. It
      works the same whether the row sits in the dashboard's own{" "}
      <code>Dogs</code> section or the settings screen's dogs table. It
      used to refuse there and point you at <code>s</code> for settings
      instead; now the row itself is enough. Inside the pane,{" "}
      <code>e</code> is the field list's own edit key, the same as on a
      sheep's config; only <code>Esc</code> closes it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The form comes from the dog. Shep asks the binary for its schema when
      the pane opens rather than reading one it stored, because{" "}
      <code>cargo install</code> replaces a dog with nothing watching and a
      stale schema mislabels which field is a credential. A built-in dog
      answers without being spawned at all. A dog that publishes no schema
      gets no pane, and a line saying where its settings do live:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock>{`pydog publishes no schema; edit dogs.toml with $EDITOR`}</CodeBlock>
    <Callout variant="careful">
      Asking runs it. Opening the pane on an adopted dog spawns that binary
      with <code>--schema</code>, waits a second for an answer, then kills
      it. That is a third party's code executing because you pressed a key,
      and it is worth knowing before you browse the config of a dog you did
      not write. A built-in dog is shep's own binary and is never spawned.
    </Callout>
    <p>
      The pane is flat, with no section headers: a dog's schema carries no
      groups, and inventing some would be shep deciding how somebody else's
      config reads. The cost column is empty on every row, because shep does
      not know what a dog's field costs. The foot of the pane says so once,
      naming the dog:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock>{`shep publishes the change; bark decides what to reload`}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      A field the dog marks as a credential reads <code>&lt;set&gt;</code>{" "}
      and never its value, and the editor on it opens empty. Typing a new one
      replaces it; nothing here can show you what it replaced. A table or a
      list gets <code>~</code>, the same glyph a sheep's{" "}
      <code>args</code> gets and for the same reason.
    </p>
    <p>
      Edits file into the same change set a sheep's pane uses, and{" "}
      <code>u</code> undoes them the same way; nothing reaches the dog's
      section until <code>Esc</code> closes the pane. However many fields
      changed, the write sends the section whole, in one request, so the
      comments and key order in <code>dogs.toml</code> survive it. Applying
      an empty value removes the key, which puts the dog back on its own
      default. Neither <code>*</code> nor <code>!</code> appears: a dog has
      no Flockfile to differ from, and <code>dogs.toml</code> is the one
      copy of its settings.
    </p>
    <p>
      The explanation panel draws here too, at the same widths, with the
      dog's own help text, current value, default and example; only the
      impact sentence is missing, since shep doesn't know what a dog's
      field costs. There's no tab row: a dog's schema carries no groups to
      move between.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      <code>r</code> re-reads the section and not the schema, so a keystroke
      whose job is to re-read a file never respawns somebody else's binary.
    </p>

